using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace HCMv3.Navigation;

/// <summary>
/// The initial screen catalogue: every routable menu item in the application, with the Screen Code it
/// was first assigned.
///
/// GENERATED ONCE, THEN THE TABLE IS AUTHORITATIVE. This list seeds rows that do not exist yet; it never
/// renumbers or overwrites one that does. That distinction is the whole design. Codes get printed in
/// training material and quoted in support tickets, so a code that silently changed because someone
/// inserted a menu item would invalidate all of it. A new screen takes the next free number in its
/// prefix; an admin may re-map a code deliberately, and that edit survives every future run.
///
/// KEY VERSUS CODE. <c>ScreenKey</c> is derived from the route ONCE and is immutable thereafter. The
/// route was the only unique identifier available -- four menu items share a name, no two share a route.
/// Once seeded the key stops tracking the route: move a screen and only <c>Route</c> changes. Favourites
/// reference the key and never the code, so re-mapping a code cannot break them.
///
/// Prefixes follow the agreed scheme: S for Screen, then the module, then three digits -- SPA001,
/// SPY001, SRPT001. Three digits everywhere rather than two, because Reports alone has 147.
/// </summary>
public static class ScreenCatalog
{
    public record ScreenSeed(
        string Key,
        string Code,
        string Module,
        string NameKey,
        string Route,
        string PermissionName);

